blocks.body:
For sets $A, B$ inside some universe $U$:

- union $A \cup B = \{x : x \in A \text{ or } x \in B\}$;
- intersection $A \cap B = \{x : x \in A \text{ and } x \in B\}$;
- difference $A \setminus B = \{x : x \in A \text{ and } x \notin B\}$;
- complement $A^c = U \setminus A$;
- Cartesian product $A \times B = \{(a,b) : a \in A,\ b \in B\}$, the set of ordered pairs.

Union, intersection and complement are the set versions of "or", "and" and "not"; the product is how we build the pairs that relations are made of. If $A$ and $B$ are finite, $|A \times B| = |A|\cdot|B|$.

A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is

- reflexive if $a \mathrel{R} a$ for every $a \in A$;
- symmetric if $a \mathrel{R} b$ implies $b \mathrel{R} a$;
- antisymmetric if $a \mathrel{R} b$ and $b \mathrel{R} a$ together imply $a = b$;
- transitive if $a \mathrel{R} b$ and $b \mathrel{R} c$ together imply $a \mathrel{R} c$.

In the arrow picture: reflexive means a loop at every dot, symmetric means every arrow has a return arrow, antisymmetric means no two distinct dots have arrows both ways, transitive means every two-step path has a shortcut arrow.

blocks.solution:
By the theorem above it is enough to count partitions of a 4-element set, grouped by block sizes:

- one block $\{1,2,3,4\}$: 1 way;
- sizes 3+1: choose the singleton, 4 ways;
- sizes 2+2: the block containing 1 has one partner among 3 elements, 3 ways;
- sizes 2+1+1: choose the pair, $\binom{4}{2} = 6$ ways;
- four singletons: 1 way.

The total is $1 + 4 + 3 + 6 + 1 = 15$. (These counts are the Bell numbers $1, 1, 2, 5, 15, 52, \dots$; compare this with the $2^{16} = 65536$ relations of all kinds on the same set.)

