blocks.body:
A proposition is a statement that is either true (T) or false (F), such as "7 is prime" (T) or "$2+2=5$" (F). "$x > 3$" is not a proposition until we know $x$. Letters $p, q, r$ stand for propositions, and connectives build new ones:

- negation $\neg p$ ("not $p$"): true exactly when $p$ is false;
- conjunction $p \wedge q$ ("$p$ and $q$"): true exactly when both are true;
- disjunction $p \vee q$ ("$p$ or $q$"): true when at least one is true (the inclusive or);
- conditional $p \to q$ ("if $p$ then $q$"): false only when $p$ is true and $q$ is false;
- biconditional $p \leftrightarrow q$ ("$p$ if and only if $q$"): true when $p$ and $q$ have the same truth value.

The conditional surprises people. Think of $p \to q$ as a promise: "if it rains, I will bring an umbrella". The promise is broken only if it rains and I do not bring one. If it does not rain, the promise is kept whatever I do. So $p \to q$ is true whenever $p$ is false; this is called vacuous truth. For example "every element of $\varnothing$ is negative" is true.

A truth table lists the value of a compound formula for each of the $2^n$ assignments of T/F to its $n$ letters. Here is the table for the conditional:
$$\begin{array}{cc|c} p & q & p \to q \\ \hline T & T & T \\ T & F & F \\ F & T & T \\ F & F & T \end{array}$$

An argument with premises $\varphi_1, \dots, \varphi_k$ and conclusion $\psi$ is valid if $\psi$ is true under every assignment that makes all the premises true; equivalently, $(\varphi_1 \wedge \dots \wedge \varphi_k) \to \psi$ is a tautology. Validity is about form: a valid argument with a false premise may have a false conclusion. The most used rules of inference are

- modus ponens: from $p$ and $p \to q$, conclude $q$;
- modus tollens: from $\neg q$ and $p \to q$, conclude $\neg p$;
- hypothetical syllogism: from $p \to q$ and $q \to r$, conclude $p \to r$;
- universal instantiation: from $\forall x\, P(x)$, conclude $P(c)$ for any particular $c$; existential generalisation: from $P(c)$, conclude $\exists x\, P(x)$.

Two classic invalid patterns are affirming the consequent (from $q$ and $p \to q$, conclude $p$) and denying the antecedent (from $\neg p$ and $p \to q$, conclude $\neg q$). The assignment $p = F$, $q = T$ defeats both.

