Fix the following logical axioms (all instances of these schemes, and all generalisations $\forall x_1 \cdots \forall x_n\, \alpha$ of them):

1. every instance of a propositional tautology, such as $\alpha \to (\beta \to \alpha)$;
2. $\forall x\, \alpha \to \alpha^x_t$, where $\alpha^x_t$ replaces the free occurrences of $x$ by a term $t$ that can be substituted without any of its variables becoming bound;
3. $\forall x\, (\alpha \to \beta) \to (\forall x\, \alpha \to \forall x\, \beta)$;
4. $\alpha \to \forall x\, \alpha$, when $x$ does not occur free in $\alpha$;
5. $x = x$, and $x = y \to (\alpha \to \alpha')$ for atomic $\alpha$, where $\alpha'$ is obtained from $\alpha$ by replacing $x$ by $y$ at zero or more (not necessarily all) of its occurrences.

The only rule is modus ponens: from $\alpha$ and $\alpha \to \beta$, infer $\beta$. A deduction of $\varphi$ from $\Gamma$ is a finite sequence of formulas ending in $\varphi$, each of which is in $\Gamma$, is a logical axiom, or follows from two earlier ones by modus ponens. We write $\Gamma \vdash \varphi$ if one exists, and call $\Gamma$ consistent if there is no $\varphi$ with both $\Gamma \vdash \varphi$ and $\Gamma \vdash \neg \varphi$.

This is essentially the calculus in Enderton's textbook, where the schemes are numbered as six groups (the two equality schemes separately); many equivalent systems exist (natural deduction, sequent calculi), and the theorems below hold for all the standard ones. Two basic facts about it are used constantly: the deduction theorem, $\Gamma \cup \{\alpha\} \vdash \beta$ if and only if $\Gamma \vdash \alpha \to \beta$; and $\Gamma$ is inconsistent if and only if $\Gamma \vdash \varphi$ for every $\varphi$.

