blocks.body:
A theory $T$ is a set of sentences in a first-order language; its theorems are the sentences $\sigma$ with $T \vdash \sigma$. We work in the language of arithmetic with symbols $0, S, +, \cdot, <$, intended to be read in the standard model $\mathbb N$. The theory $T$ is

- consistent if there is no sentence $\sigma$ with $T \vdash \sigma$ and $T \vdash \neg \sigma$;
- complete if for every sentence $\sigma$, $T \vdash \sigma$ or $T \vdash \neg\sigma$;
- effectively axiomatised if there is an algorithm that decides, for any sentence, whether it is an axiom of $T$;
- sound if every theorem of $T$ is true in $\mathbb N$.

Two standard theories: Robinson arithmetic $Q$, with a few simple axioms such as $\forall x\, (Sx \ne 0)$ and $\forall x\, \forall y\, (x + Sy = S(x+y))$, and Peano arithmetic PA, which adds the induction scheme: for each formula $\varphi(x)$, the axiom $\big(\varphi(0) \wedge \forall x\, (\varphi(x) \to \varphi(Sx))\big) \to \forall x\, \varphi(x)$.

"Complete" here is a property of a theory, unlike the completeness theorem, which is about the logic.

